    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Explore optimizing the dense row-wise DP without allocating the N×N blocked matrix: store blocked coordinates compactly by row, then retain the O(N²) recurrence while skipping/efficiently marking blocked cells. Consider cache-friendly row buffers and parallelizing independent setup only; the DP dependencies across rows and columns constrain parallelism. Benchmark and preserve exact modulo/count behavior for arbitrary valid blocked inputs, including blocked endpoints.",
          "B": "Investigate a sparse-obstacle combinatorial method: sort blocked coordinates (including start/end sentinels), compute unobstructed monotone paths with binomial coefficients modulo 1e9+7, and subtract paths through earlier obstacles using inclusion-exclusion DP. Validate coordinate orientation, blocked endpoints, duplicate obstacles, and factorial/inverse-factorial limits against the baseline on all cases. Compare time/memory with O(N²) dense DP; target ARM64, four cores, no GPU.",
          "C": "Study parallelization of the row recurrence using obstacle sparsity, while maintaining exact results for every input. A scalable alternative is to exploit sparse blocked-cell constraints with path-count combinatorics; benchmark it against the existing O(N²) DP and independently validate edge cases (N=1, blocked start/end, duplicates, no obstacles). Avoid assumptions about the generated test distribution and ensure modular arithmetic is safe.",
          "G": "Integrate the strongest independently validated candidate(s) from A/B/C, measuring the actual integrated program rather than assuming gains add. Baseline is correct on 4 cases, mean 0.08119 s, score 0.5, on ARM64/4 cores/no GPU. Prioritize reducing the baseline's O(N²) memory and work: it allocates an N×N bool grid and scans every cell. Preserve input/output and correctness for all valid inputs; benchmark repeated runs and retain only measured improvements. Do not modify tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source outside allowed candidate work.",
          "analysis": "Problem D is the active source: baseline uses O(N²) blocked-cell storage and O(N²) serial DP, though M is constrained sparse for large N. A sparse obstacle combinatorial approach can reduce cost to O(M²) naively, but must be benchmarked and may be inferior when M is dense at smaller N; sparse row storage plus recurrence is a safer distinct avenue. No automatic profiling beyond 4-case correctness and 0.08119 s baseline is provided, so recommendations are hypotheses to measure, not presumed wins. Preserve general correctness, including endpoint blocks and coordinate handling."
        },
        "request_sha256": "1b13a74b67954661a1323af4894fdc8c61254f62daf88a6d74afee365bf81742",
        "response": {
