    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Develop a low-overhead dense DP: one contiguous row updated in place, conditional subtraction instead of remainder, and no row-copy pass. Replace the quadratic blocked-cell bitmap with sorted, deduplicated obstacle coordinates processed by row; evaluate obstacle-free spans against compact bitmap lookup. Accelerate input parsing without changing accepted inputs. Preserve blocked start/end behavior and n=1. Measure complete executable time.",
          "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm. Sort and deduplicate blocked cells; use factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1000000007 to count unrestricted paths, then subtract paths whose first blocked cell is each preceding reachable obstacle. This costs O(n+m²) time and O(n+m) storage and includes a general obstacle-free binomial solution. Retain a dense fallback for large obstacle counts. Establish a measured crossover rather than assuming the sparse algorithm always wins.",
          "C": "Research four-core ARM64 parallelism through segmented modular prefix scans. Within each row, unblocked cells form prefix sums of the previous row, while blocked cells reset the running sum. Explore chunk-local scans followed by carry propagation that stops at the first obstacle in each chunk, using persistent workers and bounded modular arithmetic. Compare synchronization and extra passes against serial DP; retain a serial path when overhead dominates. Use only facilities supported by the unchanged build environment.",
          "G": "Integrate only correctness-validated programs from A/B/C. Measure each candidate independently, then selective combinations, retaining the fastest validated complete program rather than assuming additive gains. Consider a runtime dispatcher based on dimensions and deduplicated obstacle count, with measured cost thresholds and a universally correct fallback. Check duplicate obstacles, blocked endpoints, n=1, whole-row barriers, and modular overflow within the existing validation process. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or encode expected answers.",
          "analysis": "The supplied source implements Problem D; A/B/C here are research roles, not the other contest problems. Its principal costs are O(n²) cell updates, quadratic blocked-cell storage, modular remainder, and row copying. The only measured evidence is a correct baseline across four cases, averaging 0.08113 seconds; input sizes and bottleneck profiles are unavailable. The target has four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU, so startup and synchronization overhead may matter. Dense-kernel improvement, sparse combinatorial counting, and segmented parallel scanning offer distinct research directions. All must preserve exact modular counts for every valid input; no benefit or crossover is established yet."
        },
        "request_sha256": "788cc6590d7c6b1f4b06caa5f4c55abcf8f3710b0d4a22d7b6d4be20b38a227a",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Develop a low-overhead dense DP: one contiguous row updated in place, conditional subtraction instead of remainder, and no row-copy pass. Replace the quadratic blocked-cell bitmap with sorted, deduplicated obstacle coordinates processed by row; evaluate obstacle-free spans against compact bitmap lookup. Accelerate input parsing without changing accepted inputs. Preserve blocked start/end behavior and n=1. Measure complete executable time.",
            "B": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle algorithm. Sort and deduplicate blocked cells; use factorials and inverse factorials modulo 1000000007 to count unrestricted paths, then subtract paths whose first blocked cell is each preceding reachable obstacle. This costs O(n+m²) time and O(n+m) storage and includes a general obstacle-free binomial solution. Retain a dense fallback for large obstacle counts. Establish a measured crossover rather than assuming the sparse algorithm always wins.",
