      "mother_plan:synthesis": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "synthesis",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research an exact sparse-obstacle alternative: coordinate-sorted inclusion–exclusion with factorial binomials, but only when its O(m²) cost is demonstrably preferable to O(n²) rolling DP. Focus on safe hybrid dispatch, including m=0/direct binomial and blocked endpoints; do not tune to individual tests.",
          "B": "Build from the fastest measured program (b87aa26, 0.02207 s). Investigate lower-overhead ARM64-friendly four-or-larger-lane segmented prefix scans, obstacle-row storage, and buffered input while preserving modular arithmetic and all boundary/duplicate-obstacle behavior. Keep a simple scalar tail/reference path for validation.",
          "C": "Independently investigate whether persistent 4-core parallel segmented scans can beat the best serial-vector implementation on genuinely wide dense workloads without harming smaller cases. Emphasize correct carry propagation across chunks and a principled runtime dispatch; prior OpenMP candidates (0.02727/0.02986 s) are useful baselines, not assumed improvements.",
          "G": "Integrate only measured, correct candidates. Start from b87aa26, retain its sparse per-row storage and one-row vectorized scan, then compare whole-program variants rather than assuming gains compose; reject slower/higher-risk merges and verify endpoints, n=1, duplicate blocks, empty rows, and arbitrary valid obstacle layouts.",
          "analysis": "The baseline is 0.07984 s. The best admissible result is B b87aa26 at 0.02207 s; its one-row segmented DP avoids the original quadratic bitmap and its SIMD scan outperformed both OpenMP variants. Sparse combinatorial hybrids are valid opportunities for low obstacle counts but the measured A versions are slower (0.05427/0.06241 s), so they require strict cost-based dispatch. Integration should prioritize B and empirical whole-program measurement."
