    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Implement the safest parallel baseline: distribute independent target-body indices `i` across up to the target’s 4 CPU cores, using static scheduling. Preserve ascending `j` accumulation and the existing arithmetic; synchronize between force and position updates and between simulation iterations. Measure on all supplied cases and verify correctness before comparing speed.",
          "B": "Research a data-layout optimization: unpack positions (and, if useful, velocities) from the binary `Body` records into separate coordinate arrays for the 10 iterations, then repack the original six-float-per-body format for output. Keep the force calculation and each body’s `j` traversal order unchanged; avoid unsafe math or reordered reductions. Include transcode costs in measurements.",
          "C": "Prototype a target-tiled force kernel that computes several independent target bodies together, with each target retaining the original ascending `j` order and its own force accumulators. Use compiler-supported SIMD where possible, optionally with contiguous coordinate arrays, and retain a scalar fallback. Measure it independently and reject any variant that fails correctness.",
          "G": "Integrate only candidate programs produced by A, B, and C. Preserve binary I/O, the 10 iterations, softening, and the original computation for arbitrary valid inputs. Check correctness on every supplied case, then measure each promising candidate and each combined program with the existing harness; select by measured results, not assumed additive gains. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
          "analysis": "The only measurement is a valid baseline covering 4 cases, with mean runtime 0.660540 s (samples 0.660371–0.660841 s); there are no candidate results or prior interactions to guide selection. The provided source is an O(n²) force calculation repeated for 10 iterations. Its outer target-body loop is independent, making it a low-risk parallelization opportunity on the 4-core ARM64 target. Separate layout and target-lane SIMD experiments explore complementary CPU optimizations while preserving per-body accumulation order and the binary interface."
        },
