    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Research cheaper successor generation while keeping visited-state semantics and traversal unchanged. First eliminate box sorting on walking moves and repeated goal scans when no box moved; check the initial state separately. For pushes, replace bubble sorting with local insertion that reproduces the existing sortable prefix exactly—the current loop excludes the final box slot. Then compare bounds-checked precomputed neighbors and a cell-to-box-index occupancy map built once per expanded state against the current repeated scans. Use sparse clearing or generation stamps rather than clearing the whole board for every successor. Preserve candidate order, exact player position, push legality and output case. Do not assume enclosing walls, fixed dimensions or a fixed number of boxes. Measure these changes separately before combining them; occupancy setup and preprocessing may outweigh savings on small puzzles. Hand off the best independently validated variant with official timings and an explanation of why its state sequence remains equivalent.",
          "B": "Research compact exact-state storage and duplicate detection. Establish a cheap control variant that checks the stored full hash before memcmp. The main structural experiment is box-layout interning: hash and collision-check the complete source-equivalent box vector, then represent each search node by layout ID, exact player cell, parent ID and move. Walking successors reuse their layout; only pushes construct another box vector. Compare per-layout visited-player bitsets with sparse storage where dense allocation would be excessive. Never replace the exact player position with a reachable-region representative. Allocate permanent path nodes only after duplicate rejection, and preserve stable IDs and alignment during growth. Frontier arrays must reproduce the reference's prepend-per-level traversal, first-parent selection and goal-on-discovery timing, not ordinary FIFO behavior. Preserve box-slot ordering and verify full keys after hash collisions. Measure initialization, resizing and memory overhead as part of end-to-end time; retain the simpler control if interning loses.",
          "C": "Research sound deadlock pruning without changing minimum-player-move BFS. Existing live marking already rejects cells lacking any static reverse-push route to a goal. First evaluate push-triggered 2-by-2 wall/box freeze detection, rejecting only fully blocked patterns containing an off-goal box. Separately evaluate per-goal reverse-push reachability, including the required pusher-support cell, followed by an injective box-to-goal matching feasibility check. Failure to match every box is a necessary-condition rejection, not a heuristic guess. Cache results by collision-checked box layout, independently of player position, and avoid rerunning checks on walks. Preprocessing and matching may cost more than they save at this runtime, so compare each pruning stage independently. Supply a soundness argument for every rejection rule. Preserve the relative order of surviving successors and ensure successful-state handling is not defeated by an empty frontier. Do not substitute push-count optimization, speculative deadlock patterns or player-region merging.",
          "G": "Integrate programs supplied by A/B/C, retaining the selected baseline and each best standalone candidate. Require exact correctness results, official end-to-end timings and stated equivalence or pruning arguments before integration. Measure compatible pairwise combinations before considering a triple combination, within the existing budget. Remeasure every merged artifact: gains are not additive, and the best standalone program may remain the winner. Reconcile representation changes carefully, especially occupancy indices, interned layouts, pruning caches, parent IDs and frontier ordering. Audit first-discovery duplicate handling, goal-check timing, hash-collision equality, the reference's partial box ordering, allocation growth, solved/exhausted-frontier behavior and exact output reconstruction. Four passing correctness cases do not establish equivalence for all valid inputs. Treat improvements comparable to baseline timing scatter cautiously, using only permitted measurements. Keep tests, oracle, compiler, target and budgets unchanged; select the most defensible measured winner, retaining the baseline if no candidate improves reliably.",
          "analysis": "The target is src/sokoban.c, not the other problems in the supplied statement. Baseline-e1 passed four correctness cases and averaged 4.777 ms across three timing samples, ranging from 4.649 to 4.962 ms. No profiling or optimized variants are supplied. Source-level opportunities are repeated box scans, copying/sorting on walks, chained hashing without a full-hash equality filter, and pointer-heavy state storage. These are hypotheses, not measured bottlenecks. Favor low-overhead serial work initially on the four-core ARM64 target; threading overhead and deterministic duplicate resolution are unproven at this runtime. Correctness includes minimum player moves, output case and tie selection. The stated u,d,l,r preference differs from the source's r,l,u,d generation with prepended level lists, and its sort excludes the final box slot. Do not silently treat FIFO replacement or full box canonicalization as behavior-preserving. All proposals must remain general and use the unchanged correctness oracle."
        },
        "request_sha256": "9898ca3af7282e4628d90e9fb52972231edd1e12ff1dd45795901651aeaaa3ee",
        "response": {
