    "mother_plans": {
      "mother_plan:preparation": {
        "model_calls": 1,
        "phase": "preparation",
        "plan": {
          "A": "Develop the low-risk characteristic-polynomial replacement. With p=1000000007, use q(x)=x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN. Compute r(x)=x^(H-1) modulo q and return sum(r[i]*A[i]) modulo p, using zero-based A. This gives O(N^2 log H) time and O(N) workspace. Evaluate left-to-right exponentiation: polynomial squaring followed, for each set bit, by multiplication by x with O(N) reduction; avoid unnecessary general polynomial multiplications. Preserve coefficient orientation during descending-degree reduction and handle N=1 correctly. Use dynamically sized, reusable buffers rather than matrix objects. Also produce an exact ring-buffer recurrence path for short horizons D=H-N, with selection based on generic operation costs and this run's measurements—not particular test values. Deliver a complete solver and evidence from the unchanged evaluator.",
          "B": "Develop an independent Bostan–Mori solver. Define Q(x)=1-P1*x-...-PN*x^N and R(x)=((A1+A2*x+...+AN*x^(N-1))*Q(x)) modulo x^N. The answer is coefficient H-1 of R/Q. For index m, form U=R(x)*Q(-x) and V=Q(x)*Q(-x); retain U coefficients whose parity equals m&1, retain V's even coefficients, and halve m. At m=0 return R[0]/Q[0]; Q[0] remains 1 with this normalization. Start with exact quadratic convolution. Then exploit parity directly: writing Q(x)=E(x^2)+x*O(x^2), its updated denominator is E(y)^2-y*O(y)^2. Similarly avoid computing discarded numerator coefficients. Compare this regular, parity-reduced O(N^2 log H) method against A rather than assuming equal asymptotics imply equal performance. Audit zero coefficients, degree drops, modular subtraction, and indexing without changing the tests.",
          "C": "Research reusable exact convolution and modular-arithmetic kernels, supplying a complete candidate based on B's formulation. Prioritize compact 32-bit residues, widened 64-bit products, scratch-buffer reuse, squaring symmetry, and bounded delayed reduction. For example, at most 16 ordinary products plus a reduced accumulator fit in uint64_t; count doubled off-diagonal contributions twice. Never accumulate an entire unrestricted dot product in 64 bits. As a higher-risk experiment, evaluate three-prime NTT/CRT convolution against schoolbook multiplication. N<=1020 requires transforms of at most 2048 points for B's full products. Auxiliary primes such as 998244353, 1004535809, and 469762049 have a product exceeding the coefficient bound 1021*(p-1)^2, permitting exact reconstruction before reduction modulo p. Two such primes are insufficient for that bound; direct radix-two NTT modulo p is unsuitable. Retain schoolbook below a measured crossover. Explore up to four workers only where the unchanged build already supports them and measurements justify overhead; use independent convolution outputs or auxiliary-prime transforms, not dependent reduction steps. Avoid architecture-specific x86 assumptions.",
          "G": "Integrate only programs and kernels actually produced and validated by the research trees. Establish each standalone candidate's correctness and timing under the unchanged evaluator before combining anything. Compare A and B as alternative general engines; then separately evaluate A's short-horizon path and C's arithmetic or convolution kernels in the strongest engine. Use only mathematically valid, generic dispatch rules with measured crossovers. Preserve a serial path for small workloads. Audit one-based sequence indexing, P1 multiplying the most recent term, N=1, zero coefficients, maximum residues, arithmetic bounds, and exactly one output residue. Four reported correctness cases are not a proof of general correctness. Require automatic measurements for the actual integrated artifact: improvements are not additive, and an integration may lose to either input. Retain the fastest verified standalone candidate if necessary. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
          "analysis": "The active problem is NPbonacci. The source exponentiates an N-by-N companion matrix using O(N^3 log(H-N)) work, reduces every multiply-add, and finally performs a full matrix multiplication despite the base having only one populated column. Three fixed-size matrix objects reserve approximately 48 MiB, with a potentially additional by-value result temporary. Computing one recurrence term does not require dense matrix powers. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.0196524 seconds across three close samples. No measured input dimensions or hotspot profile are supplied, so neither maximum-size behavior nor a startup bottleneck can be inferred. A and B therefore investigate distinct exact recurrence algorithms, while C targets their arithmetic kernels. The short observed runtime favors cautious, measured use of threading on the four-core ARM64 CPU; no GPU is available."
        },
        "request_sha256": "631a020b3f4381f67e1bbc985b31bd6c6a88b418cc7e48e03461443475b61b4e",
        "response": {
          "data": {
            "A": "Develop the low-risk characteristic-polynomial replacement. With p=1000000007, use q(x)=x^N-P1*x^(N-1)-...-PN. Compute r(x)=x^(H-1) modulo q and return sum(r[i]*A[i]) modulo p, using zero-based A. This gives O(N^2 log H) time and O(N) workspace. Evaluate left-to-right exponentiation: polynomial squaring followed, for each set bit, by multiplication by x with O(N) reduction; avoid unnecessary general polynomial multiplications. Preserve coefficient orientation during descending-degree reduction and handle N=1 correctly. Use dynamically sized, reusable buffers rather than matrix objects. Also produce an exact ring-buffer recurrence path for short horizons D=H-N, with selection based on generic operation costs and this run's measurements—not particular test values. Deliver a complete solver and evidence from the unchanged evaluator.",
            "B": "Develop an independent Bostan–Mori solver. Define Q(x)=1-P1*x-...-PN*x^N and R(x)=((A1+A2*x+...+AN*x^(N-1))*Q(x)) modulo x^N. The answer is coefficient H-1 of R/Q. For index m, form U=R(x)*Q(-x) and V=Q(x)*Q(-x); retain U coefficients whose parity equals m&1, retain V's even coefficients, and halve m. At m=0 return R[0]/Q[0]; Q[0] remains 1 with this normalization. Start with exact quadratic convolution. Then exploit parity directly: writing Q(x)=E(x^2)+x*O(x^2), its updated denominator is E(y)^2-y*O(y)^2. Similarly avoid computing discarded numerator coefficients. Compare this regular, parity-reduced O(N^2 log H) method against A rather than assuming equal asymptotics imply equal performance. Audit zero coefficients, degree drops, modular subtraction, and indexing without changing the tests.",
            "C": "Research reusable exact convolution and modular-arithmetic kernels, supplying a complete candidate based on B's formulation. Prioritize compact 32-bit residues, widened 64-bit products, scratch-buffer reuse, squaring symmetry, and bounded delayed reduction. For example, at most 16 ordinary products plus a reduced accumulator fit in uint64_t; count doubled off-diagonal contributions twice. Never accumulate an entire unrestricted dot product in 64 bits. As a higher-risk experiment, evaluate three-prime NTT/CRT convolution against schoolbook multiplication. N<=1020 requires transforms of at most 2048 points for B's full products. Auxiliary primes such as 998244353, 1004535809, and 469762049 have a product exceeding the coefficient bound 1021*(p-1)^2, permitting exact reconstruction before reduction modulo p. Two such primes are insufficient for that bound; direct radix-two NTT modulo p is unsuitable. Retain schoolbook below a measured crossover. Explore up to four workers only where the unchanged build already supports them and measurements justify overhead; use independent convolution outputs or auxiliary-prime transforms, not dependent reduction steps. Avoid architecture-specific x86 assumptions.",
            "G": "Integrate only programs and kernels actually produced and validated by the research trees. Establish each standalone candidate's correctness and timing under the unchanged evaluator before combining anything. Compare A and B as alternative general engines; then separately evaluate A's short-horizon path and C's arithmetic or convolution kernels in the strongest engine. Use only mathematically valid, generic dispatch rules with measured crossovers. Preserve a serial path for small workloads. Audit one-based sequence indexing, P1 multiplying the most recent term, N=1, zero coefficients, maximum residues, arithmetic bounds, and exactly one output residue. Four reported correctness cases are not a proof of general correctness. Require automatic measurements for the actual integrated artifact: improvements are not additive, and an integration may lose to either input. Retain the fastest verified standalone candidate if necessary. Do not alter tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
            "analysis": "The active problem is NPbonacci. The source exponentiates an N-by-N companion matrix using O(N^3 log(H-N)) work, reduces every multiply-add, and finally performs a full matrix multiplication despite the base having only one populated column. Three fixed-size matrix objects reserve approximately 48 MiB, with a potentially additional by-value result temporary. Computing one recurrence term does not require dense matrix powers. The baseline passed four cases and averaged 0.0196524 seconds across three close samples. No measured input dimensions or hotspot profile are supplied, so neither maximum-size behavior nor a startup bottleneck can be inferred. A and B therefore investigate distinct exact recurrence algorithms, while C targets their arithmetic kernels. The short observed runtime favors cautious, measured use of threading on the four-core ARM64 CPU; no GPU is available."
          },
